{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1320,
    "dDurationMs": 3700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our sun and the earth, and all the planets and moons and dwarf planets"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5070,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and asteroids and comets... The Solar System in short"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8110,
    "dDurationMs": 3400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "formed about 4.6 billion years ago from a nebulous cloud"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11530,
    "dDurationMs": 3810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of swirling gas and dust which coalesced thanks to the irresistibly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15340,
    "dDurationMs": 1700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "attractive force of gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17290,
    "dDurationMs": 4110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, this nebula started off more or less as a big shapeless blob."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21400,
    "dDurationMs": 2380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So how did our solar system end up with all the planets"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23820,
    "dDurationMs": 2250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and their moons orbiting in a flat disk?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26150,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I mean, we've all seen the planetary model of the atom,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28650,
    "dDurationMs": 2570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is definitely wrong when applied to atoms."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31220,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it also kind of suggests that planets might revolve"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33660,
    "dDurationMs": 1730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around the sun every which way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35390,
    "dDurationMs": 2900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So is our solar system somehow special in its flatness?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38330,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or is the planetary model of the atom doubly wrong?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41270,
    "dDurationMs": 2830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, our solar system definitely isn't alone."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44100,
    "dDurationMs": 2420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many exoplanets' star systems are flat,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46520,
    "dDurationMs": 1590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a lot of galaxies are flat,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48200,
    "dDurationMs": 1950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole accretion disks are flat,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50230,
    "dDurationMs": 1220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Saturn's rings are flat,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51500,
    "dDurationMs": 970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "etc."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52470,
    "dDurationMs": 3410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So why, when there's all of 3D space to fill,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55970,
    "dDurationMs": 2690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "does the universe have this preference for flatness?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58690,
    "dDurationMs": 2020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The answer has to do with two things:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60710,
    "dDurationMs": 3090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collisions and the fact that we live in three dimensions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63870,
    "dDurationMs": 640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bear with me."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64610,
    "dDurationMs": 4550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Anytime a bunch of objects held together by gravity are zooming and circling around,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69260,
    "dDurationMs": 3350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their individual paths are nearly impossible to predict."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72610,
    "dDurationMs": 2670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And yet, collected together, they have a single total amount"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75340,
    "dDurationMs": 2120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that they spin about their center of mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77560,
    "dDurationMs": 3580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It may be hard to figure out exactly what direction that rotation is in,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81140,
    "dDurationMs": 2780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but the mathematics implies there must be some plane"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84000,
    "dDurationMs": 2600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in which the cloud, taken as a whole, spins."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86600,
    "dDurationMs": 3170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, in two dimensions, a cloud of particles rotating in a plane"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89770,
    "dDurationMs": 2950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is flat by definition, it's in two dimensions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92760,
    "dDurationMs": 3620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in three dimensions, even though the rotation of the cloud is given"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96380,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by one plane, particles can whiz around far up and down from that plane."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100770,
    "dDurationMs": 2830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As the particles bump into each other, all the up and down motion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103640,
    "dDurationMs": 3500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tends to cancel out. It's energy lost in crashing and clumping."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107220,
    "dDurationMs": 3160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yet, the whole mass must continue spinning inexorably,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110440,
    "dDurationMs": 3730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because in our universe, the total amount of spinning in any isolated system"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114210,
    "dDurationMs": 1450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "always stays the same."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115720,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So over time, through collisions and crashes, the cloud loses its loft"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119460,
    "dDurationMs": 5990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and flattens into a spinning, roughly 2 dimensional disk shape, like a solar system or a spiral galaxy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125450,
    "dDurationMs": 3400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, in 4 spacial dimensions, the math works out such that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128870,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there can be two separate and complementary planes of rotation,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132290,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is both really really hard for our 3D-thinking brains to picture"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135810,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and also means there's no up and down direction in which particles lose energy by collisions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140710,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So a cloud of particles can continue being just that... a cloud."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 144290,
    "dDurationMs": 3990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And thus, only in three dimensions can a nebula or infant galaxy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148340,
    "dDurationMs": 2430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "start out not flat and end up flat"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150810,
    "dDurationMs": 3650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is definitely a good thing, because we need all that matter to clump together"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154460,
    "dDurationMs": 2730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in order for stars and planets to form, and for us,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 157190,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even those of us who think atoms look like this,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159530,
    "dDurationMs": 1340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to exist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162360,
    "dDurationMs": 2660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This episode of minute physics is supported in part by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165020,
    "dDurationMs": 3500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University, one of our subscribers"
    } ]
  } ]
}
